00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,849
New towns sprouted along the rail lines almost overnight, allowing for more new settlers, known as homesteaders,

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:14,220
from the eastern states and Europe to flood onto the Great Plains, transforming it into sprawling acres of productive farmland.

3
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:20,480
But the area to the west of the Mississippi was still home to a myriad of Native American tribes,

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,420
who viewed the ever-increasing number of settlers encroaching onto their ancestral land as a direct threat to their ancient way of life.

5
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:34,280
Throughout the latter half of the 19th century, the rapid expansion, settlement

6
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,439
and often lawless development of the American frontier made conflict

7
00:00:38,719 --> 00:00:40,960
between natives and settlers an inevitability.

8
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:49,119
The United States Army conducted a decades-long campaign to pacify and forcibly remove the tribes of the Great Plains

9
00:00:49,119 --> 00:00:52,880
and southwest, with hundreds of engagements being fought across the region.

